\cf1 You are getting confused about the main point and implementation of what is so poor about OS/8 [and only because of DTORG].\line\line The whole idea about mass storage devices in general are they are more reliable as a group than something simpleminded like paper-tape formats.\line\line But more importantly, paper-tape formats can be grossly inefficient serving no proper purpose; that said, DTORG perverted the design which is already incompatible, making it worse!\line\line First, a history of DEC BIN format:\line\line 1) BIN format defined 4K loading.  This is some loader earlier than the LBAA release and its immediate predecessor.  LBAA came out in 1967 so that it wouldn't be corrupted if you are using TC01 DECtape.  The changes are done by hand in the write up so we can see what the immediate predecessor was, but even that one already supports extended memory.\line\line Thus, we can only presume there was an even earlier one that had only the ability to load into a 4K machine, etc.\line\line Call this Version 1.0.  It has leader and trailer that have the high-channel bit punched only, and is ignored when loading, and loading stops when the trailer is detected.\line\line The words are double-buffered because we may encounter the last two frames which is NOT loading, so we have to defer loading until we are sure this is NOT yet the case.  So, we get a new word [two adjacent frames, they create a 12-bit word from the six low-order bits, move six over and add the next six not moved.  If the first one has the bit just to the next of the data set [which is one to the right of the high-bit, then this word is an origin setting for loading the NEXT word.  If not set, then this is data to LOAD and then add one to the loading pointer [instead of loading a new value into it].  one bit determines everything because there are only those two possibilities.\line\line Notice so far this is also RIM format, but RIM MUST have an origin setting before every loadable word because the feeble RIM loader lacks the concept of the increment-the-load-pointer function, etc.\line\line And of course RIM doesn't have the checksum, so it is not double-buffered [also does not support memory field changes.]\line\line All of the "live" frames are paired off but are checksummed frame at a time.  this means that the I-am-an-origin-setting bit is factored into the checksum.  [If you did it 12-bits at a time, that would never happen, so in essence you are doing it 7 bits at a time because by definition the high-bit is always off.  But it is just carried out arithmetically, it just works out that way, etc.  [If you AND.ed off the high-order bit nothing would change because it is always 0 except in leader/trailer]\line\line OK, then after the fact they added on FIELD change.  The format is both highest bits are both punched.  The low-order bits are the field in the range of 0 to 7 [although that means expansion is possible for the future, such as the KT8A where it might somewhat matter!]  the three bits to the left of the lowest are unused in classic BIN format.  I doubt anyone with a KT8A is demanding a KT8A-aware paper-tape loader!  Yet, PAL8 is outputting that format internally!\line\line Field settings DO NOT contribute to the checksum.  I suspect because that would break the oldest loader code, which I doubt any of us will ever see, etc.\line\line Otherwise, there would be no reason to exclude it, and theoretically adding it into a running checksum is marginally more reliable, so it must have been excluded deliberately for a reason.\line\line In any case, in the code of a working BIN loader, the FIELD setting s changed to a CDF x0 instruction where x is the field in the low-order bits.  Then, when a DCA I LOADPTR is about to be executed, first execute the CDF x0 to the loading field. [Note, no other instruction in the BIN loader is indirect!]\line\line Remember, ALL MRI except JMP or JMS use the DF when indirect, those two use the instruction field thus you need to have a CIF x0 instruction just before either of them, etc.  not relevant to BIN loaders.\line\line That's LBAA from may 1967 except they had to patch it to NOT use 07754 and 07755 because those are the TC01/08 word count and current address reserved locations.  Running DECtape kills them, and that destroys the older loader, but the hand-made patches in the write-up fixup that.  [But leave it wide open to the DF32/RF08 analogous locations 07750 and 07751.\line\line Remember, P?S/8 will fix ALL such problems with all three 3-cycle Data break devices, thus will be the only one bug-free.\line\line Currently it can be broken if you load TC58 magtape programs because it need 07752/07753 but that's fixed in the next release of BIN the P/s/8 utility program NOT DEC Bin the paper-tape format and loaders, etc.\par
\par
