So,it is even stupider than I gave them credit for.  But remember, they had no clue what a machine larger than 4K was!  That's why the broken type 552 controller was palatable to them to help DEC palm them off on customers.  the ECO was set the break field on the type 552 controller to the DATA FIELD.  But that means you cannot have interrupts on, because an interrupt saves the DF an IF THAT WAS and clears both of them!\line\line Of course, if they had interrupts on strictly 4K machines, they didn't care!\line\line Also, the TU55 [the "solid state" DECtape drive] was able to be made compatible with "relay logic levels" to work with the 552 instead of the 555 drives, etc.\line\line So, Typeset-8 customers paid top dollar to have palmed off on them old crappy junk that was never finished because they were designed for the PDP-5 and they literally FORGOT about extended memory!\line\line In any case, this is what ruins BIN format\line\line Now to how OS/8 fails to protect itself from this:\line\line Instead of an INTELLIGENT format such as Slurp, the morons made Richard Lary use images of paper-tape for everything!  Each file consists of 256 12-bit word records that are arranged as 384 frames that have to be unpacked.  that's fine for ASCII files, and I intend the same for the SHELL text format [with even some extensions], but thoroughly stupid for a loader for 12-bit words.\line\line Thus, OS/8 .BN files become corrupted when DTORG [their way] is used and ABSLDR will ALWAYS get an error attempting to load OS/8 binary files with DTORG within, etc.\line\line So, not only is it inefficient, it is now perverted.\line\line There is no point in having OVERHEAD of paper-tape inside of a file.  Slurp is equivalent but 12-bit word oriented.  NOT 8-bit byte frames from a paper-tape [with arbitrary short leaders and trailers inside of the file].\line\line Thus, no one SHOULD be storing those frames, but OS/8 DOES, and inherently storage devices store DATA in their native hardware format as required.  Devices are more reliable and it's just plain silly to have an RK8E that creates CRC-32 protection have the overhead of leaders and trailers and checksum frames inside of the file thus you need an unpack level "in front" of a loader [which is why it is so slow, and I beat loading speed with SLURP on a DECtape when they used ABSLDR on an RK8E/RK05.\line\line So, don't compare apples to orange crates!\par
