  3.\tab Binary Compatibility\par
\par
\tab In general, binary compatibility refers to the principle that,\par
\tab to the extent possible, sections of one program are identical to\par
\tab the corresponding sections of another program derived from the\par
\tab first program.\par
\par
\tab When one program is created from another program (through the use \par
\tab of overlays or other means), maintaining binary compatibility\par
\tab guarantees that additional program modifications or patches\par
\tab designed for the first program will be functional when applied to \par
\tab the corresponding sections of the second program.\page\cf5 Glossary of Terms (continued)\cf0\line\line\tab P?S/8 FOCAL\sub 99\nosupersub  is based on FOCAL, 1969\sub 99\nosupersub ; all modifications made\par
\tab to support FOCAL, 1969\sub 99\nosupersub  maintain binary compatibility to the\par
\tab fullest extent possible.  This guarantees support for patches\par
 \tab created by members of the FOCAL\sub 99\nosupersub  user community at large.\par
\par
\tab Failure to maintain binary compatibility is a serious problem\par
\tab of other releases such as FOCAL-8\sub 99\nosupersub  (aka FOCAL-71\sub 99\nosupersub ); this release\par
 \tab also exhibits additional problems beyond binary compatibility\par
\tab (related to deliberate attempts at sabotaging products made by a\par
\tab competitor).\par
