  9.\tab Data Break\par
\par
\tab Nearly all DEC\sub 12\nosupersub  computers support Direct Memory Access\sub 99\nosupersub  (DMA\sub 99\nosupersub ) \tab peripheral devices.  Many DEC\sub 12\nosupersub  engineering documents refer to \tab this data transfer method as Data Break.\par
\par
\tab All PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  models (with the exception of the LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub ) support \tab Data Break peripheral devices.  A distinction is made between \tab single-cycle and three-cycle Data Break devices:\par
\par
\tab a) Single-cycle Data Break Devices transfer data between the\par
 \tab    device and memory in a single cycle per 12-bit word\par
 \tab    transferred.  The peripheral device is responsible for\par
\tab    management of all data transfers.\par
\par
\tab b) Three-cycle Data Break devices designate hard-wired memory\par
\tab    addresses reserved to manage data transfers; this tends to\par
 \tab    standardize the Data Break operations and simplify the\par
 \tab    peripheral device implementation.\page\cf5 Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\cf0\par
\tab    For example, the TC01\sub 99\nosupersub  DECtape\sub 99\nosupersub  controller reserves memory\par
\tab    locations 07754-07755.  During a typical transfer, the\par
 \tab    contents of 07754 (known as the Word Count Register) are set\par
 \tab    to the two's complement of the desired number of words to\par
 \tab    transfer.  The contents of 07755 (known as the Current\par
 \tab    Address Register) are set to one less than the designated \par
\tab    memory address for the data transfer.\cf5\par
\cf0\par
\tab    Note: The memory field for the Data Break is set by separate\par
 \tab    hardware within the peripheral device generally known as the\par
 \tab    Break Field Register.\par
\cf2\par
\cf0\tab    Once the data transfer is initiated by software, each data\par
 \tab    transfer proceeds when the controller is ready to obtain\par
\tab    multiple memory cycles from the CPU\sub 99\nosupersub . The first transfer\par
 \tab    operation increments the Word Count Register; if the contents\par
\tab    changes from 7777 -> 0000 a condition known as Word Count\par
\tab    Overflow occurs, which ends the entire Data Break transfer\par
\tab    operation.\par
\par
\tab    Assuming Word Count Overflow does not occur, the next transfer\par
\tab    operation increments the contents of the Current Address\par
\tab    Register; in conjunction with the Break Field Register, a data\par
\tab    transfer operation is initiated on the third cycle.  A 12-bit\par
\tab    data word is transferred between the controller and the\par
\tab    designated memory location.  Word Count Overflow (eventually)\par
\tab    occurs ending all data transfers; this can be detected by the\par
\tab    transfer initiating program (which continues execution when\par
\tab    Data Break operations are not in effect).  In most Data Break \tab    peripherals, the Word Count Overflow (and any associated\par
\tab    errors) can be detected by designated flag and interrupt\par
\tab    conditions.\par
\par
\tab Depending on peripheral bus design, various converters may be\par
\tab required to interface specific devices to newer computer models.\cf5\par
\cf0\par
\tab Certain OMNIBUS\sub 99\nosupersub  peripherals perform additional DMA cycles as \tab part of their operational design.  For example, the DP8E\par
\tab synchronous modem interface can check each data word transferred \tab for a character match against as many as four possible values.\par
\tab This simplifies the implementation of various synchronous data\par
\tab protocols which require special attention when specific control \tab characters are detected.\par
\par
\tab Note: All DECmates\sub 99\nosupersub  (past the DECmate I\sub 99\nosupersub ) do not support Data\par
