In any case, here is a simplified view of it just for you:\line\line\line P?S/8 for any device that must need extended-memory extension-based system device handlers is generally at a disadvantage because of a lazy cheat that only works in OS/8.\line\line The worst case example is all RX and as a consequence all DECmate II and up stuff.\line\line OS/8 has so much stuff reserved in field 1 7600-7777 that all you have to do is get 17666 and up loaded from that major fraction of the first half of record 0.  The handler is pristinely loaded not overlayed directly into 07600-07777 and it has nothing to do with getting itslef in.  That's thus, the transient copy of that first part of the first-half of record 0.  When looked upon as DECtape, the boot block only has to load block 1 into 07600 and said code does no work, it just gets placed,\line\line But P?S/8 gets it all done in ONE BLOCK.\line\line As such, what typically happens is that part of the block is reserved with overhead words from the RX boot series, and worst of all, some of them are NOT overlayed.  That means those relative words MUST be literally a copy of the designated bootstrap [which also stagnated the design of the bootstrap itself!]\line\line As such, P?S/8 block 0 is incomplete with a "big hole" in it with regard to what relatively wants to be in 07600 for a working handler.\line\line The RX50 makes this even worse because some of the words that are NOT CODE but rather reserved STORAGE words, such as having the date in relative 007 and 010 [now that the date is 18 bits] and some others MUST be able to withstand the entire problem both statically and dynamically.\line\line Thus, what happens is that the boot process is now just to get into memory and started one of the two "helper" blocks defined as part of the total of TEN extra blocks, 8 for the handler extension, etc.\line\line Once THAT code is in memory, it relocates the part of the handler that was allowed to be what it was supposed to be to the relavent part of 07600-07777 as intended [Note, the code is loaded relative to location 0002! Not 0000, but 0002!  So, it is all off by two as well!  the bootup code was selfishily and expendiently done, etc.\line\line Then after that's done, there is another copy of what belongs there also in the helper blocks.  That code is then loaded where needed.\line\line At this point, the basic handler is loaded, and all that is still needed is "only" to load the 8 pages into x6000-up where x is the highest field. [this is essentially what the RX01 handler does].\line\line the differennce is there are many small problems to solve, and it has to do all of them in only 8 pages.\line\line Some of them require internal buffers because the 128 word blocks have to be buffered because it is 8 pages in 3 records if we support byte ode, and 8 pages in 4 records otherwise, which is a little easier to do.\line\line In any case, "stealth" techniques have to be used to ensure that attemlts to access block 0 are filtered properly for validity, cause errors if too much is wrong.  Writes only update the sacred words, and they are not even on block 0, they are written to one of the two helper blocks and supplied into the caller's buffer when reading along with whatever else is in the contents of block 0 otherwise.  When writing, only the sacred words are updated.  this will work even with BLKCPY doing a write, read back and compare operation, etc.\line\line But no actual block 0 writing occurs.\line\line Which brings up yet another problem.  Version 9A needs not only non-system handlers to be used to create the system, but there has to be a special WRITe RAW unsecured function to get the first half of the boot sector written as it needs to be exactly.  This is part of my notion of asking for double-precision blocks numbers starting with the high-order bits all set.  Maybe something like ask for block 77777740 means write raw on what is block 0 bypassing the normal filtering and protection, etc.  On systems where none of this matters, write and write raw would be identical, etc.\line\line And then of course, all the low-level stuff to calculate the first time initial track and sector, and later derive the logical next already having the current one.  this is right out of the RX01 conceptually, but these are 10 sector disks so you need an EAE simulator type of divide not by 13, but rather divide by 5, etc.\line\par
Just mimicking OS/8 you have to do the RK kind of fixups because the records are two pages long in 12-bit mode, so all of that happens as well, except there is no magic half-sector mode, so all four cases have to be handled, yet be prepared to not have to do anything speical for well-aligned normal reads and writes away from block 0 so the performance holds up.\line\line I'm probably missing a few things, but each of them is a tiny little bitch of a problem to solve!\par
