\cf4  16.\tab DECtape\par
\par
\tab DECtape is a removable media storage device based on the earlier\par
\tab LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub  used on the (classic) LINC\sub 99\nosupersub , LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub  and PDP-12\sub 99\nosupersub  as\par
\tab an embedded subsystem of these machines.\par
\par
\tab Note: While the media used for both DECtape and LINCtape\sub 99\nosupersub  is\par
\tab nominally the same magnetic material and tape width, the two\par
\tab formats differ greatly.  The most obvious difference is that when\par
\tab using DECtapes, the supply reel is mounted on the left side of\par
\tab the tape drive; when using LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub , the supply reel is\par
\tab mounted on the right side of the tape drive.  Forward motion for\par
\tab either device is defined as winding the tape media onto the\par
\tab takeup reel (mounted on the other side of the drive).\par
\par
\tab Where applicable, plastic canisters for tape media storage exist.\par
\tab By convention, blue plastic canisters are used for DECtapes while\par
\tab green plastic canisters are used for LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub .  (This\par
\tab convention is often violated.)\page Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\tab DECtapes, much like LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub , are individually block-\par
\tab replaceable devices and can be considered to be analogous to more\par
\tab typical rotating disk drives; however, block placement is far\par
\tab more critical due to the inherent overhead of locating a selected\par
\tab block by traversing all intervening blocks starting from the\par
\tab present tape position.\par
\par
\tab Additionally, there are considerations regarding the choice of\par
\tab initial search direction: Using the wrong initial direction will\par
\tab cause undue delays because of the need to turn the tape around\par
\tab after performing a sufficient undershoot (which may have been\par
\tab avoidable).  As such, more successful operating systems use\par
\tab performance-enhancing techniques when accessing the storage\par
\tab device; less efficient operating systems may take profoundly\par
\tab longer time to accomplish analogous tasks.\line\line\tab A case in point regarding DECtape is the ability to transfer\par
\tab multiple blocks in the same device handler call; once the current\par
\tab block has been transferred in the latest read or write operation, \tab starting a new device handler call must restart the overall\par
\tab operation for the latest desired block (because the motion bit\par
\tab must be cleared at the end of the latest transfer).  Assuming the\par
\tab next desired block is adjacent to the last block transferred\par
\tab previously, the delay in completing the next transfer will be\par
\tab palpable.  As such, more successful software strategies combine\par
\tab multiple block transfers in the same device handler operation to\par
\tab the extent possible.\par
\par
\tab LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub  can only transfer data moving in the forward\par
\tab direction.  Tapes in either format can search while moving in\par
\tab the reverse direction; however, DECtapes can also transfer data \tab once the desired block is located.  Certain software utilities\par
\tab take advantage of reverse motion data transfers, especially on\par
\tab certain DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  machines based on various word lengths.\par
\par
\tab Unlike LINCtapes\sub 99\nosupersub , the count of words in a DECtape block is\par
\tab subject to a restriction of being a whole multiple of 18-bit\par
\tab words.  As such, the standard format for the PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  is 129 12-\par
\tab bit words (or 86 18-bit words).\par
\par
\tab Note: The underlying format of DECtape (first used on the PDP-1\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\tab and PDP-4\sub 99\nosupersub ), was changed with the release of the type 552\sub 99\nosupersub  \tab DECtape controller originally designed for the \i PDP-5\sub\i0 99\nosupersub .  As such, \tab DECtapes for the PDP-1\sub 99\nosupersub  and PDP-4\sub 99\nosupersub  are merely compatible with\par
\tab both systems and cannot share data with other machines that\par
\tab support DECtape.  (A tentative solution to this problem is\par
\tab available on the LINC-8\sub 99\nosupersub )\page Glossary of Terms (continued)\par
\par
\tab For detailed information on other DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  machines, the reader is\par
\tab directed to various documents on early computer architecture; the\par
 \tab PDP-8\sub 99\nosupersub  is the first successful DEC\sub 14\nosupersub  12-bit computer based on\par
\tab two's complement arithmetic (which is used throughout the\par
\tab computer industry on virtually all newer systems).\par
\par
\tab With the exception of very early Typeset-8\sub 99\nosupersub  computers, all\par
\tab PDP-8\sub 99 \nosupersub systems equipped with DECtape use a compatible DECtape\par
\tab controller as follows:\par
\par
\tab a) TC01\sub 99\par
\par
\nosupersub\tab b) TC08\sub 99\nosupersub\line\line\tab c) TD8E\sub 99\par
\nosupersub\par
\tab DECtape drives used with the above DECtape controllers are as\par
\tab follows:\line\par
\tab a) TU55\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\par
\tab b) TU56\sub 99\nosupersub\par
\tab\par
\tab Notes for this section:\par
\par
\tab a) The TU55\sub 99\nosupersub  is a single drive that can be set to select as any\par
 \tab    drive in the range of 0-7.\par
\par
\tab b) The TU56\sub 99\nosupersub  is generally a dual drive where each drive can be\par
